course: dictionary
lesson: 10491
title: Turnout: how many people come
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Turnout is a noun. It means how many people come to an event, or how many people vote in an election. «Turnout means how many people actually voted.» Look for clue words in the sentence, such as come, vote or election, to show which meaning is used.
explain: Use the phrases to check your meaning. A good turnout means many people came. A low turnout means few people came. At school, the turnout at a concert or a match tells you how popular it was.
explain: Watch for the other meanings. In old-fashioned British English, a turnout can mean the way someone is dressed. In the USA, a turnout is a place at the side of a road where cars can pull over. The words around the word tell you which meaning is right.
fact: Turnout means how many people take part in an event or vote in an election.
fact: «Turnout means how many people actually voted; in many elections only about half to two-thirds of the people who could vote do so.»
fact: Common phrases are a good turnout, a low turnout, voter turnout and turnout was high.
fact: Turnout is a noun, so you can say one turnout or two turnouts.
fact: In old-fashioned UK English, turnout can mean the way someone is dressed. In the USA, it can mean a place at the side of a road where cars can pull over.
exercise: «The school concert had a good turnout, with almost every family there.» What does turnout mean here? | The song they sang | *The number of people who came | The clothes everyone wore | A place to park the cars
exercise: «Voter turnout was low in the local election.» What does voter turnout mean? | The names of the winners | *The number of people who voted | The number of candidates standing | The price of a vote
exercise: A driver stopped at a turnout beside the road to let the lorry pass. What is a turnout here, in the USA sense? | An old-fashioned way of dressing | A crowd at a football match | A kind of election | *A place at the side of a road where cars can pull over
test: «Turnout was high, so the hall was full.» Turnout here means? | Everyone left early | Nobody came at all | The hall was newly painted | *Many people came
test: «Only a low ___ came to the meeting, so the chairs stayed empty.» Which word fits the gap? | turnover | upturn | turnip | *turnout
exam: Ella says turnout is always a bad word. Is she right? | *No, a good turnout means many people came, and a low turnout means few | Yes, it always means something bad | Yes, it only ever means clothes | No, it always means a place by a road
